📅 Day 28 – DAO Pattern & Layered Architecture

Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session introduced the DAO (Data Access Object) pattern and the concept of layered architecture in Java applications. The mentor explained how these design approaches help organize large applications and improve code maintainability.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ DAO (Data Access Object) Pattern

The DAO pattern is used to separate database access logic from business logic in an application.

Key advantages discussed:

Improves code organization

Makes the application easier to maintain

Allows easier modification of database logic

Promotes reusability of data access methods

DAO classes are responsible for performing CRUD operations on the database.

2️⃣ Layered Architecture

The mentor explained how applications are structured using multiple layers to separate responsibilities.

Common layers include:

✅ Presentation Layer

Handles user interaction

Displays information to users

Example: UI, web pages, or controllers

✅ Business Logic Layer

Contains application logic and rules

Processes user requests and performs calculations

✅ Data Access Layer (DAO Layer)

Communicates directly with the database

Performs data operations such as insert, update, delete, and retrieve

🔹 Practical Implementation

During the practice session, I explored simple examples that demonstrated:

Creating DAO classes for database operations

Separating database logic from business logic

Interacting with the database through DAO methods

This helped me understand how large-scale Java applications are structured for better scalability and maintainability.